WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:04.232
This strip, in Cape Town,
South Africa, 

00:00:04.232 --> 00:00:09.102
divides the beachside community of Strand
from the township of Nomzamo.

00:00:09.102 --> 00:00:15.193
They're only a few meters apart. But the
people on each side live very different lives. 

00:00:15.193 --> 00:00:19.205
Strand has backyards and driveways.

00:00:19.205 --> 00:00:21.965
Nomzamo is much more dense.

00:00:21.965 --> 00:00:25.819
And the people here
have fewer basic services:

00:00:25.819 --> 00:00:27.980
Less piped water.

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:30.775
Less internet access.

00:00:30.775 --> 00:00:37.865
And Nomzamo is majority Black,&nbsp;
while the area across the line is majority white.

00:00:37.865 --> 00:00:44.182
If we use dots on a map to represent race,
you can&nbsp;see how stark that divide is. 

00:00:44.182 --> 00:00:49.159
If we zoom out to the whole city,
we can see it's actually everywhere.

00:00:49.159 --> 00:00:53.339
And this is the case
across much of South Africa.

00:00:53.339 --> 00:00:57.570
The color of your skin here
often determines where&nbsp;you live.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:00.647
It also determines
your quality of life.

00:01:00.647 --> 00:01:06.480
This map shows where jobs and opportunities&nbsp;
are primarily concentrated in Cape Town.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:09.614
And this is where most of
the city's Black&nbsp;people live,

00:01:09.614 --> 00:01:13.991
in informal settlements called "townships"
on the city's periphery.

00:01:13.991 --> 00:01:19.057
"People&nbsp;have to move by public transport
for&nbsp;up to three hours a day,

00:01:19.057 --> 00:01:22.413
and they can't take care&nbsp;of
their obligations in the community,

00:01:22.413 --> 00:01:24.799
with the&nbsp;rest of their family,
because they're always&nbsp;working

00:01:24.799 --> 00:01:26.800
and they're always traveling."

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:29.121
For decades, South Africa
was under apartheid:

00:01:29.121 --> 00:01:32.126
a system that wrote
segregation into law.

00:01:32.146 --> 00:01:37.778
A white minority controlled where
non-white people could live,&nbsp;work, exist. 

00:01:37.778 --> 00:01:42.080
Many were forced out of their homes.

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:48.039
In 1994, a democratically elected government
took&nbsp;power, and ended apartheid. &nbsp;

00:01:48.039 --> 00:01:51.171
It was supposed to be&nbsp;a new beginning.

00:01:51.171 --> 00:01:54.800
But a lot of the&nbsp;country still looks like this. 

00:01:54.800 --> 00:02:01.773
And that's because South Africa's legacy&nbsp;
of racial division goes back centuries.

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:09.745
In the 1600s, the Dutch took over
the southern&nbsp;tip of Africa,

00:02:09.745 --> 00:02:13.560
to supply ships with food
along the trade route to Asia.

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:18.347
150 years later, Britain seized&nbsp;it,
and named it "Cape Colony." 

00:02:18.347 --> 00:02:21.842
Many Dutch colonists
moved here, further inland,

00:02:21.842 --> 00:02:26.480
to escape British&nbsp;rule and
continue exploiting enslaved people.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:31.280
Just like the Dutch, the British used Cape&nbsp;Colony
as a strategic location for trade;

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:34.274
it wasn't economically significant.

00:02:34.274 --> 00:02:40.530
But in the 1870s, that changed, when
the British started mining diamonds there.

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:48.274
Suddenly, Cape Colony was one of Britain's
most&nbsp;prized and exploited colonies.

00:02:48.274 --> 00:02:51.998
In order to get the&nbsp;diamonds
out of the country, they built railways,

00:02:51.998 --> 00:02:55.600
to connect the mines up here
to the coast.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:59.311
The railways allowed the British
to access a global&nbsp;diamond market

00:02:59.311 --> 00:03:02.400
through the port city of Cape Town.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:06.928
Soon, the economy of Cape Colony
was centered&nbsp;around the railroads.

00:03:06.928 --> 00:03:10.634
Especially this main route.

00:03:10.634 --> 00:03:14.515
The green areas on this map show 
the Black&nbsp;regions of Cape Colony,

00:03:14.515 --> 00:03:17.568
largely left out
of the railroad economy.

00:03:17.568 --> 00:03:24.005
Racial inequality in&nbsp;Cape Colony
was being reinforced by location.

00:03:24.005 --> 00:03:28.820
To keep it that way, the colonial government&nbsp;
started writing segregation into law. 

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:34.298
The Natives Land Act of 1913
pushed Black people into these areas:

00:03:34.298 --> 00:03:37.440
only eight percent of South Africa's land;

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:41.827
and restricted them from
owning land everywhere else;

00:03:41.827 --> 00:03:47.332
or, relocated them to the edges of
the major cities, to work for white&nbsp;people. 

00:03:47.332 --> 00:03:50.560
These laws began to shape the region.

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:57.920
Cape Town's growth from the increased trade&nbsp;
turned the port town into a major city.

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:03.040
Many migrants from the rest of the&nbsp;
colony, and elsewhere, moved here,

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:05.678
to what&nbsp;was then the
outskirts of Cape Town,

00:04:05.678 --> 00:04:10.200
where&nbsp;former enslaved people,
merchants, artists,&nbsp;and immigrants,

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:14.243
were forming a neighborhood
called&nbsp;District Six. 

00:04:14.243 --> 00:04:17.906
As the city grew around District Six,
so did the neighborhood.

00:04:17.906 --> 00:04:23.619
For decades, District&nbsp;Six was
a thriving, integrated community.

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:31.505
"We were a very cosmopolitan,
you could say&nbsp;family, almost.

00:04:31.505 --> 00:04:35.973
Because there were people
from all different nationalities,

00:04:35.973 --> 00:04:37.954
from all different walks of life."

00:04:37.954 --> 00:04:42.312
"This was the statement:
Your child&nbsp;is my child."

00:04:42.312 --> 00:04:46.092
But it wouldn't last.

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:53.892
In 1934, Britain's legal hold in what&nbsp;was now
the Union of South Africa&nbsp;officially ended.

00:04:53.892 --> 00:04:58.632
The remaining white minority, the descendants
of Dutch colonists, took control.

00:04:58.632 --> 00:05:02.960
And they built on the foundation
the British were leaving behind.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:06.359
Between 1949 and 1971,

00:05:06.359 --> 00:05:12.459
the all-white government&nbsp;passed
148 laws solidifying apartheid.

00:05:12.459 --> 00:05:16.603
"Apartheid allowed for the
full realization of the ambition

00:05:16.603 --> 00:05:19.280
of the fascist project
in South Africa."

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:25.545
In 1950, the Population Registration Act
officially classified people by race:

00:05:25.545 --> 00:05:31.375
white, colored, and native (or Black).
And eventually, Asian.

00:05:31.375 --> 00:05:34.738
Then they made laws&nbsp;saying
where people could live. 

00:05:34.738 --> 00:05:39.144
Around the country,&nbsp;Black South Africans
were moved into these areas,

00:05:39.144 --> 00:05:42.051
called homelands, or "bantustans."

00:05:42.051 --> 00:05:46.373
Bantustans were rural areas
and had underdeveloped economies.

00:05:46.373 --> 00:05:51.799
Many&nbsp;of them were in the areas Britain had
already excluded from the railway economy,

00:05:51.799 --> 00:05:56.379
and where Black&nbsp;land ownership
had been restricted to.

00:05:56.379 --> 00:05:58.801
Black people&nbsp;were forced
to carry "pass books,"

00:05:58.801 --> 00:06:04.793
that specified&nbsp;where they
were allowed to work or travel to.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:09.637
In cities like Cape Town, the "Group Areas Act"
moved&nbsp;the remaining non-whites

00:06:09.637 --> 00:06:11.942
into separate urban areas.

00:06:11.942 --> 00:06:16.260
"The most prime land, and the land
closest to higher-valued property,

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:18.692
was allocated to white people."

00:06:18.692 --> 00:06:26.230
In 1966, the government declared that
District&nbsp;Six was now a whites-only area. 

00:06:26.230 --> 00:06:30.128
The residents&nbsp;of District Six received
removal letters like&nbsp;this one,

00:06:30.128 --> 00:06:34.761
that said living there was illegal,
because they were not white.

00:06:34.761 --> 00:06:40.240
Bulldozers drove&nbsp;into District Six,
and razed it to the ground.

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:44.883
"We lived here. We had a life here."

00:06:44.883 --> 00:06:47.498
"It was very traumatic for a lot of people."

00:06:47.498 --> 00:06:51.120
"It's like ripping out someone's heart."

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:56.480
More than 60,000 people were
forcibly removed from their homes.

00:06:56.480 --> 00:07:02.488
This kind of violence against non-white people&nbsp;
was commonplace around the country.

00:07:02.488 --> 00:07:07.961
But, after decades of pressure,
both from within South Africa&nbsp;and abroad,

00:07:07.961 --> 00:07:11.819
apartheid rule finally came to an end.

00:07:11.819 --> 00:07:15.360
The new government lifted restrictions
on where people could live.

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:20.302
Millions of people, who had been&nbsp;excluded
from economic development&nbsp;for centuries,

00:07:20.302 --> 00:07:26.429
migrated to major cities, looking&nbsp;for
basic services and economic opportunity.

00:07:26.429 --> 00:07:29.468
"For any family with
no prospect of employment,

00:07:29.468 --> 00:07:34.257
the&nbsp;most rational, logical choice to make
is to migrate&nbsp;to an urban center."

00:07:34.257 --> 00:07:36.150
They settled where there was&nbsp;empty land,

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:41.119
creating townships on the peripheries
of major cities like Cape Town.

00:07:41.119 --> 00:07:46.704
The government&nbsp;built millions of homes,
and expanded clean water&nbsp;and electricity.

00:07:46.704 --> 00:07:52.034
"But it had a number of unforeseen consequences,
the most important of which&nbsp;is that

00:07:52.034 --> 00:07:57.822
the only land that could be&nbsp;used for the public
housing program&nbsp;was on the periphery of the city.

00:07:57.822 --> 00:08:03.010
And for that&nbsp;reason, a brilliant intention
to overcome the apartheid legacy

00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:08.880
unintentionally reproduced&nbsp;the very
same legacy it was trying to undo."

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:13.097
Today, 60% of the mostly Black
population&nbsp;of Cape Town

00:08:13.097 --> 00:08:16.415
lives in these townships
at the edge of the city.

00:08:16.415 --> 00:08:20.654
The thing is, Cape Town's City Center&nbsp;
has land to develop.

00:08:20.654 --> 00:08:23.944
But because of its location, it's valuable,

00:08:23.944 --> 00:08:28.794
so it usually gets sold to private
developers, who build luxury apartments. 

00:08:28.794 --> 00:08:33.423
Nearly a&nbsp;billion dollars worth of them
are going up&nbsp;by the coast.

00:08:33.423 --> 00:08:38.003
But, right in the heart of Cape&nbsp;Town,
by all the expensive developments,

00:08:38.003 --> 00:08:42.480
District Six remains largely untouched.

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:46.053
The former residents have fought
against private&nbsp;development,

00:08:46.053 --> 00:08:49.111
and they've actually succeeded.

00:08:49.111 --> 00:08:52.960
Some have even managed to return
to houses&nbsp;built by the city.&nbsp;

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:58.061
"I wanted to come back&nbsp;here,
where I was born,

00:08:58.061 --> 00:09:02.754
which was part of
our family's heritage."

00:09:02.754 --> 00:09:06.129
"I couldn't believe that I was back.

00:09:06.129 --> 00:09:09.219
It was a sense of relief."

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:15.797
But there are still hundreds of claimants
waiting&nbsp;to get back to District Six.

00:09:21.938 --> 00:09:25.023
"We haven't done the difficult
and the painful work

00:09:25.023 --> 00:09:29.677
to confront&nbsp;what the
intergenerational consequences are

00:09:29.677 --> 00:09:32.685
of colonialism. Of apartheid."

00:09:32.685 --> 00:09:38.852
The story of Cape Town&nbsp;and South Africa's
racial segregation starts far in the past.

00:09:38.852 --> 00:09:42.223
But it's very much entangled
with the&nbsp;present. 

00:09:42.223 --> 00:09:46.110
Apartheid and colonialism here are over.

00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:52.000
But many of the barriers they&nbsp;built
have yet to be dismantled.

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:56.400
"The kind of psychic scars that's left&nbsp;
on individuals and on communities.

00:09:56.400 --> 00:10:03.840
We haven't begun the work of saying, How do
we&nbsp;live together, in the face of that history?"

